### S3.3 The descent step and TERMINATION: LEMMA HE6R1-1, and the erratum the widening forces on HE7

HE7's THEOREM HE7.C states the jump condition as "a side with **ℓ ≥ 2**
carrying a residual factor of multiplicity ≥ 2 — exactly HE6-BOX-1's
configuration", and THEOREM HE7.A(3)'s trichotomy splits on ℓ₂ = 1 vs
ℓ₂ ≥ 2 alone. Both **repeat HE6.B's enumeration error one level up**: at
ℓ₂ = 1 with a repeated factor of degree d_{r₂} ≥ 2 the level-2 α-refine
Ψ ↦ Ψ − ŝ₂n₂(λ₂) does **not** exist, because ŝ₂ would need residue
β₂ ∈ K₂(β₂) ∖ K₂.

> **FINDING HE6R1-F1 (on HE7 §S2, classification: statement over-reach at
> the jump condition; NON-PROPAGATING at n ≤ 15).** THEOREM HE7.A(3)'s
> "at a slope with a repeated residual factor and ℓ₂ = 1 the level-2
> α-refine exists" is false when the repeated factor has degree ≥ 2, and
> THEOREM HE7.C's jump condition ("ℓ ≥ 2") is correspondingly too narrow.
> Both are repaired by LEMMA HE6R1-1 with **identical bounds**. The finding
> does not propagate at the degrees HE7 claims: an ambiguity needs a
> repeated level-2 factor with ℓ₂d_{r₂} ≥ 2, hence L_{λ₂} ≥ 4, hence
> **μ₂ ≥ 4** — impossible at HE7's own μ₂ ≤ 3 scope (and at n = 8, where
> μ₂ = 2), so §S8's dictionary and the n = 8 closure are untouched.

> **LEMMA HE6R1-1 (the widened jump condition descends, with HE7.C's
> bounds).** At any level i, a node requires a level jump exactly when its
> side carries a repeated irreducible residual factor r_i (multiplicity
> m ≥ 2) with **ℓ_i·deg r_i ≥ 2**; the complementary repeated case
> (ℓ_i = deg r_i = 1) is an α-refine, finite by LEMMA HE7-8. At every jump:
>
>     μ_i ≥ L_{λ_i} ≥ m·ℓ_i·deg r_i ≥ 2·2 = 4 ,
>     μ_{i+1} ≤ deg R_{λ_i}/deg r_i ≤ μ_i/(ℓ_i·deg r_i) ≤ μ_i/2 .
>
> Hence the number J of level jumps in any history satisfies
> **J ≤ log₂ μ − 1 ≤ log₂ n − 2**, exactly as in THEOREM HE7.C; at n = 8
> there is one jump with μ₂ ≤ 2, and the first degree admitting two jumps is
> n = 16.

*Proof.* The jump condition: HE6.A decides a separable residual at every ℓ;
HE3's stage-α decides a repeated K-rational linear factor at an integer
slope (HE6 §S6.3, unchanged by r1); the complement is exactly a repeated
irreducible r with ℓ·deg r ≥ 2 (§S2's enumeration). The two displays are
then HE7.C's own computations with "ℓ ≥ 2" replaced by "ℓ·d_r ≥ 2": the
first because L_λ = ℓ·deg R_λ ≥ ℓ·m·d_r = m(ℓd_r) ≥ 4, the second because
μ₂ = deg f_S/D″ ≤ n_λ/(D′ℓd_r) = D′L_λ/(D′ℓd_r) = deg R_λ/d_r ≤ μ/(ℓd_r),
using LEMMA HE6-3(b) (n_λ = D′L_λ, (SEP)-free) for the middle step and
ℓd_r ≥ 2 for the last. Iterating gives 4 ≤ μ_J ≤ μ/2^{J−1}. Every non-jump
step either strictly decreases μ, or is an α-refine (finite, LEMMA HE7-8),
or terminates the branch at μ = 1. ∎

**Both branches of the widened box supply the descent factor ℓd_r ≥ 2 —
(iv) as ℓ ≥ 2 with d_r ≥ 1, (ii) as d_r ≥ 2 with ℓ = 1 — so the widening
costs nothing in the termination bound.** That is the reconciliation's
structural punchline: the inequality that makes the ladder finite is the
same inequality that defines the widened box.

### S3.4 LEMMA HE6R1-2 (BLOCK PROJECTION) — the composition item the widening surfaced, PROVED

The widening prompted the check "how does the level-2 read START?", and
that exposed a gap that is not about ℓ at all. THEOREM HE7.A is stated about
the block factor f_S and puts μ₂ := deg f_S/D″; HE7-BOX-4 declines to prove
the multiplicity-weighted class size |S_{λ,r}| = D′ℓ·m_r·d_r on the ground
that "σ-decision uses only the level-2 polygon of the actual f_S". But when
the block is **proper** (R_λ has another distinct factor, or P(F) has
another side), f_S is not exhibited by the read and deg f_S is not pinned by
HE6+HE7: at a non-separable R_λ the HE6.A sandwich has slack
D′ℓΣ_r(m_r−1)d_r. First live configuration: a boxed node with a proper
block needs μ ≥ 5, i.e. n ≥ 10 — inside the region COROLLARY HE7.B(i)
claims. The following lemma closes it.

> **LEMMA HE6R1-2 [r1′] (block projection).** Let f be monic on the opening
> locus with disc f ≠ 0 and Φ′ ∤ f, let (λ, r) be a level-1 label of f,
> S = S_{λ,r}, f_S the block factor (LEMMA HE7-6), g := f/f_S ∈ O[x], and
> assume **Ψ ∤ f_S** **[r1′, PE1 F-1]** — equivalently Ψ ∤ f: a root of Ψ
> is a level-2 point with level-1 label (λ, r) (LEMMA HE6-1), hence lies in
> S, so if Ψ | f then Ψ's roots are roots of f_S, and Ψ — squarefree as a
> divisor of f with disc f ≠ 0 — divides f_S. Then:
>
> **(a)** r ∤ R_λ^{(g)}, the λ-residual of g's own Φ′-development; hence for
> **every** level-2 point ξ, dv₂(g(ξ)) = ℓ·h_{F_g}(λ) — a constant c_g
> depending on (g, λ) only.
>
> **(b)** For every rational κ₂ > T₂, h_{F₂,f}(κ₂) = h_{F₂,f_S}(κ₂) + c_g,
> where h_{F₂,·} is the level-2 support function of the Ψ-adic development.
> Consequently the part of the level-2 polygon P₂(f) with slope > T₂ is the
> part of P₂(f_S) translated by c_g: same breakpoints, same slopes λ₂, same
> lengths L_{λ₂} — so
>
>     μ₂ = Σ_{λ₂ > T₂} L_{λ₂}(f)   and   |S_{λ,r}| = D″·μ₂
>
> are **computable from f**, without exhibiting f_S.
>
> **(c)** At every such side, R_{λ₂}^{f} and R_{λ₂}^{f_S} have the same
> degree L_{λ₂}/ℓ₂ and the same monic irreducible factors (same radical);
> in particular one is separable iff the other is, and in the separable
> case **they agree up to the K₂^× scalar carried by g's level-2 residue**
> **[r1′, PE1 F-3]** (a fixed γ_g ∈ K₂^×, nonzero by (a); the sealed "they
> are equal" claimed the scalar is 1, which nothing pins). The consumers
> are scale-invariant — HE7's §S1 convention reads residuals as meaningful
> up to K₂^×-scaling, and THEOREM HE7.A(2) consumes only the monic
> irreducible factors — so THEOREM HE7.A(2) may still be applied with the
> residual read off f.

> **RIDER HE6R1-2R [r1′, PE1 F-2] (LEMMA HE7-7′(b), one evaluand wider).**
> Let F ∈ O[x] be monic with Ψ ∤ F and F = Σ_j C_j^{(F)}Ψ^j its Ψ-adic
> development (deg C_j^{(F)} < D″, C_0^{(F)} ≠ 0). Then for any level-2
> point ξ with dv₂(Ψ(ξ)) = κ₂,
>
>     dv₂(F(ξ)) = h_{F₂,F}(κ₂) + δ_F(ξ) ,   h_{F₂,F}(κ₂) := min_j(dv₂(C_j^{(F)}) + jκ₂) ,
>
> with δ_F(ξ) > 0 ⟺ κ₂ is a slope λ₂ of P₂(F) and
> ι_ξ^{(2)}(R^F_{λ₂})(β₂,ξ) = 0 — the display of LEMMA HE7-7′(b) with f_S
> replaced by F.

*Proof of the rider.* Frozen HE7-7′(b) (HE7 §S5.4) states this at F = f_S
only; clauses (b) and (c) below consume it at F = f, so the wider form is
displayed rather than silently assumed. The proof is HE7-7′(b)'s with no
change: each term C_j^{(F)}(ξ)Ψ(ξ)^j has dv₂ equal to
dv₂(C_j^{(F)}) + jκ₂ exactly, by (SLOT₂) at the coefficient
(deg C_j^{(F)} < D″) and dv₂(Ψ(ξ)) = κ₂; on a κ₂-line the attaining j
satisfy j ≡ j₁ (mod ℓ₂), because the line height is an integer only then
(gcd(u₂, ℓ₂) = 1); and the tie-residue sum assembles, through the §S1
twist, to ι_ξ^{(2)}(R^F_{κ₂})(β₂,ξ). No step reads the evaluand beyond
its development-coefficient degrees and C_0 ≠ 0 — HE7 §S5.8(c)'s audit,
item (6), records precisely this mechanism for HE7-7′ (coefficients of
degree < D″ feed (SLOT₂); the congruence consumes gcd(u₂, ℓ₂) = 1 and
nothing else), so the extension re-runs a key-free, evaluand-blind proof
on a second input. ∎

*Proof.* **(a)** g is monic in O[x] (monic division by f_S), g \| f, all
roots of g are roots of f hence (T1)/(T2) points, and disc g ≠ 0. Suppose
r \| R_λ^{(g)}. LEMMA HE6-4 applied to g (its proof needs only (RES),
LEMMA HE6-1/HE6-2/HE6-2′ and LEMMA HE6-3 for g, all available for any such
g) gives a root of g with level-1 label (λ, r) — contradicting that S
collects **all** roots of f with that label and S ∩ roots(g) = ∅. So
r ∤ R_λ^{(g)}. Now let ξ be a level-2 point: dv(Φ′(ξ)) = λ and β_ξ is a root
of ι_ξ(r), so ι_ξ(r) is β_ξ's minimal polynomial over ι_ξ(K), and
ι_ξ(R_λ^{(g)})(β_ξ) ≠ 0 because r ∤ R_λ^{(g)}. LEMMA HE6-2′ applied to g
therefore gives dv(g(ξ)) = h_{F_g}(λ) with **no excess**, i.e.
dv₂(g(ξ)) = ℓ·h_{F_g}(λ) =: c_g for every level-2 point ξ. ✔

**(b)** Fix a rational κ₂ > T₂ and let r₂,₀ ∈ K₂[Z] be monic irreducible of
degree > max(deg f/D″, μ₂) with r₂,₀(0) ≠ 0 (finite fields have
irreducibles of every degree). By (LIFT₂) — available at κ₂ > T₂, §S3.2
item 2 — form Ψ₂ := Ψ₂,{κ₂,r₂,₀}; by LEMMA HE7-7 every root ξ of Ψ₂ is a
level-2 point with dv₂(Ψ(ξ)) = κ₂ and β₂,ξ of minimal-polynomial degree
> the degree of any level-2 residual of f or of f_S, so LEMMA HE7-7′(b) —
at f via RIDER HE6R1-2R **[r1′, PE1 F-2]** (Ψ ∤ f by the [r1′] hypothesis),
at f_S as frozen — gives dv₂(f(ξ)) = h_{F₂,f}(κ₂) and
dv₂(f_S(ξ)) = h_{F₂,f_S}(κ₂), both with no excess. Since valuations add along f = f_S·g and dv₂(g(ξ)) = c_g by (a),
h_{F₂,f}(κ₂) = h_{F₂,f_S}(κ₂) + c_g. Both sides are concave piecewise-linear
in κ₂ with breakpoints at the polygon slopes; two such functions differing
by a constant on (T₂, ∞) have the same breakpoints and the same slopes
there, i.e. the same sides with the same lengths. LEMMA HE7-9(a) says every
side of P₂(f_S) has slope > T₂ and THEOREM HE7.A(1) that their lengths sum
to μ₂; hence Σ_{λ₂ > T₂}L_{λ₂}(f) = μ₂ and deg f_S = D″μ₂. ✔

**(c)** Degrees: L_{λ₂}(f) = L_{λ₂}(f_S) by (b), and deg R_{λ₂} = L_{λ₂}/ℓ₂
in both cases. Factors: let r₂ ∈ K₂[Z] be monic irreducible with r₂(0) ≠ 0
and form Ψ₂ := Ψ₂,{λ₂,r₂}. Every root ξ is a level-2 point at height λ₂
whose β₂,ξ has minimal polynomial ι^{(2)}_ξ(r₂). For such ξ,
dv₂(f(ξ)) = h_{F₂,f}(λ₂) + δ_f(ξ) and dv₂(f_S(ξ)) = h_{F₂,f_S}(λ₂) + δ_S(ξ)
(LEMMA HE7-7′(b); at f via RIDER HE6R1-2R **[r1′, PE1 F-2]**); subtracting
(a) and (b) gives δ_f(ξ) = δ_S(ξ). Hence
ι^{(2)}_ξ(R^f_{λ₂})(β₂,ξ) = 0 ⟺ ι^{(2)}_ξ(R^{f_S}_{λ₂})(β₂,ξ) = 0, i.e.
r₂ \| R^f_{λ₂} ⟺ r₂ \| R^{f_S}_{λ₂} (a field embedding transports
divisibility, and β₂,ξ's minimal polynomial is ι(r₂)). As r₂ was arbitrary,
the radicals coincide; with equal degrees, separability of one forces
separability of both, and two separable polynomials with the same radical
and the same degree agree up to a scalar in K₂^× **[r1′, PE1 F-3]**. The
scalar is g's level-2 residue: residues multiply along f = f_S·g, and by
(a)'s no-excess display res(g(ξ)/n₂(c_g)(ξ)) = ι_ξ(γ_g) for a fixed
γ_g ∈ K₂^×, so the term-residue assembly gives R^f_{λ₂} = γ_g·R^{f_S}_{λ₂}
— equal exactly when γ_g = 1, which is not claimed. ∎

**[r1′, PE1 F-1] Why the Ψ ∤ f_S hypothesis was added.** The sealed
statement omitted the hypothesis this note itself discovered one section
below (FINDING HE6R1-F2), and (b) is false without it: at Ψ | f the
development's constant coefficient A₀^{(2)} vanishes, the j = 0 pin of
P₂(f) sits at ∞, and the finite-slope part above T₂ accounts for μ₂ − 1
only (one block missing — §S3.4b's own diagnosis), against (b)'s display
μ₂ = Σ_{λ₂>T₂} L_{λ₂}(f). Witnesses: the 12 Ψ | f battery members
(block = everything, g = 1, c_g = 0 — the sealed hypotheses hold there),
with the failing stratum pinned independently by the PE1 verifier's
cross-tab over the 944 members (status BADTOTAL ⟺ Ψ | f, 0 mismatches).
Structurally, (b)'s closing step consumes THEOREM HE7.A(1), which at HE7's
frozen HEAD carries the same hypothesis (§S1 CONVENTION [r1]). The cure
when Ψ | f_S is one line: peel first — LEMMA HE6R1-3 gives f_S = Ψ·f_S′
with Ψ ∤ f_S′, and the lemma applies to f/Ψ (monic, disc ≠ 0, Φ′-coprime,
with (λ, r)-block factor f_S′) with μ₂ − 1 in place of μ₂ — the same cure
R2 installed at level 1.

**Consequences (why this completes the composition).** (1) At a boxed node
the read starts from f alone: μ₂ and the level-2 sides come from
LEMMA HE6R1-2(b) (peeling first per LEMMA HE6R1-3 when Ψ | f **[r1′]**),
the residual up to its radical from (c). (2) At a
**non-separable** R_λ with several distinct factors, each distinct factor's
class size is pinned by running (b) for that factor's key:
|S_{λ,r_i}| = D′ℓd_{r_i}·μ₂^{(i)} — so **HE7-BOX-4's count is available in
READ form** (not as the closed form D′ℓm_rd_r, which stays unproved and
unneeded), and the multiplicities m_{r_i} are never consulted: the read
descends into every distinct factor's block and each descent terminates by
LEMMA HE6R1-1. (3) Since the level-2 residual of f agrees with f_S's up to
a K₂^× scalar whenever it is separable **[r1′]** — and HE7.A(2) consumes
only the monic irreducible factors — THEOREM HE7.A(2) applies with data
read off f.

### S3.4b LEMMA HE6R1-3 (THE LEVEL-2 PEEL) — found BY THE MACHINE, and it is Codex F2 one level up

The sealed battery reported 12 non-decisions out of 944 members (status
BADTOTAL: the reader's block sizes did not sum to deg f). Diagnosis: at those
members **dv₂(A_0^{(2)}) = ∞, i.e. Ψ | f** — the level-2 polygon loses its
j = 0 pin and one block goes missing. This is the **exact level-2 analogue of
Codex PE1 finding 2** (the Φ′ | f stratum at level 1, repaired at level 1 by
R2): HE7 never states the hypothesis Ψ ∤ f_S, without which
COROLLARY HE7-7″ has roots with no finite level-2 label.

> **FINDING HE6R1-F2 (on HE7 §S1/§S2/§S5.5, classification: MISSING
> HYPOTHESIS; repairable in one line, machine-discovered).** THEOREM HE7.A
> and COROLLARY HE7-7″ require **Ψ ∤ f_S**. Without it the Ψ-development's
> constant coefficient vanishes and roots shared with Ψ carry dv₂(Ψ(ρ)) = ∞,
> exactly as Φ′ | f breaks HE3-0(iii) at level 1. Repair: LEMMA HE6R1-3.

> **LEMMA HE6R1-3 (the level-2 peel).** Suppose Ψ | f_S. Then
> g₀ := gcd(f_S, Ψ) **equals Ψ**, Ψ is IRREDUCIBLE over O of degree D″, and
> it is a simple factor of f_S with
>
>     e(Ψ) = e₁ℓ ,    f(Ψ) = f₁d_r .
>
> Consequently f_S = Ψ·f_S′ with **Ψ ∤ f_S′**, every root of f_S′ is still a
> level-2 point, and THEOREM HE7.A applies to f_S′ with μ₂ − 1 in place of
> μ₂. The peel terminates (μ₂ drops by 1 each time, and in fact at most once).

*Proof.* Every root ξ of g₀ is a root of Ψ = Ψ_{λ,r}, hence a level-2 point
with level-1 label (λ, r) (LEMMA HE6-1), so LEMMA HE6-0′ gives
[L_ξ : K₀] ≥ (e₁ℓ)(f₁d_r) = D″. Since f_S ∈ O[x] and its root set is
Galois-stable, g₀ ∈ O[x] is Galois-stable too, so it contains ξ's whole orbit:
deg g₀ ≥ D″. But g₀ | Ψ gives deg g₀ ≤ deg Ψ = D″. Hence deg g₀ = D″, g₀ = Ψ,
and Ψ is a single Galois orbit, i.e. irreducible, with e ≥ e₁ℓ, f ≥ f₁d_r and
ef = D″ = (e₁ℓ)(f₁d_r), forcing equality on both. Simplicity and Ψ ∤ f_S′
follow from disc f_S ≠ 0 (a second Ψ-factor would repeat roots). ∎

**Machine confirmation.** The post-seal supplementary leg
(`verification/openmath/he6r1_supp.py`, item (A)) applies this peel to all 12
degenerate members: **12/12 decided, and all 5 PARI-checkable ones AGREE**,
each with exactly one peel and σ = {(2,2),(2,2)} = {(e₁ℓ, f₁d_r)} ⊔ {the
level-2 block of f_S′} — the lemma's prediction, member by member.

