### Dated obstruction record (2026-08-12, R7 finding 1 — LEMMA HE6R1-1's global jump bound; the A4 adjudication transcribed verbatim)

The defect stands. No dated append cures it.

HE6R1-1 itself proves only

\[
\mu_{i+1}\le \mu_i/2,\qquad \mu_i\ge4
\]

at a jump. Thus, for \(J\) jumps,

\[
4\le\frac{\mu}{2^{J-1}},
\qquad
J\le\log_2\mu-1.
\]

Globally \(n=D'\mu\) with only \(D'\ge1\), yielding

\[
J\le\log_2 n-1.
\]

The stronger \(\log_2 n-2\) bound requires \(D'\ge2\). The supplied \(D'=1,\ n=\mu=4\) example attains \(J=1\), directly contradicting the accepted display’s bound \(J\le0\).

An honest correction must either:

- replace \(\log_2 n-2\) by \(\log_2 n-1\), moving the first possible jump to \(n=4\) and two jumps to \(n=8\); or
- restrict HE6R1-1 to composite-stage starts \(D'\ge2\), leaving initial degree-one events outside its global claim.

Either change weakens the accepted displayed theorem. HEAD’s “Dated acceptance record” says the note is “ACCEPTED (2/2) and FROZEN,” and the present instruction forbids weakening accepted theorem displays. The counterexample prevents proving the existing display, so no permitted honest repair exists.

